92186362188 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "e828baa6-f3ec-5068-b0c9-1c1c3f9d8a66" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362346 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186362085 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362346 #edb/ref 1069 "q-99240" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362346 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362346 #edb/ref 1071 "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the minimum value of the function $f(x,y)=5-x+2y$ given that $2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 4.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362346 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "85f60f32-4588-5316-bc63-974d85067877" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362346 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we need to find the critical values of the function in the region's interior. We compute the critical points by solving $∇f=0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨-1,2⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the above equation has no solutions, we conclude that the function does not have critical values in the region's interior.\n\nNext, we need to find the minimum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)=2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y)\\,g(x,y)=4\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨-1,2⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨4x,2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we cannot have $\\lambda =0$ since this leads to a contradiction in the above equation. Therefore, assuming $\\lambda \\ne 0,$ we have the following\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-1 &=4\\lambda x \\\\ 2 &=2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=-\\frac{1}{4\\lambda } \\\\ y &=\\frac{1}{\\lambda }\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,x=-\\frac{1}{4}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\n  Substituting $x=-\\frac{1}{4}y$ into the condition $g(x,y)=4,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =4 \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{8}+{y}^{2} & =4 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =\\frac{32}{9} \\\\ y & =\\pm \\frac{4\\sqrt{2}}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{1}{4}y=∓\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},\\frac{4\\sqrt{2}}{3}),\\,(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},-\\frac{4\\sqrt{2}}{3}).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},\\frac{4\\sqrt{2}}{3})$ | $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},-\\frac{4\\sqrt{2}}{3})$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $5+3\\sqrt{2}$ | $5-3\\sqrt{2}$ |\n\n \n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},-\\frac{4\\sqrt{2}}{3})\\,,$ and its minimum value is $5-3\\sqrt{2}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186364649 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364650 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364649 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364651 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364649 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364652 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364649 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364653 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364649 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364654 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364649 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364649 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364649 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186364654 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364649 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "6ae58a56-6faf-52c9-9561-c87d35603ea0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364650 #edb/ref 1080 "243\\,{\\text{ft}}^{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364650 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364650 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "f027768c-f196-5e3f-adf4-7554b2177b3b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364651 #edb/ref 1080 "162\\