92186045417] [17592186446807 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186446755 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446807 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186446759 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446807 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186446773 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446807 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186446775 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446807 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446807 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446807 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186446775 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446807 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "9450a1b5-7499-5e9b-839d-b2e01f9b40ad" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446817 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186446800 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446817 #edb/ref 1069 "q-162781" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446817 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446817 #edb/ref 1071 "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the minimum value of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-4y$ given that $2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 4.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446817 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a6e0abbd-abc7-5917-a015-ff462bc321f2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446817 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we need to find the critical values of the function in the region's interior. We compute the critical points by solving $∇f=0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨2x,-4⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the above equation has no solutions, we conclude that the function does not have critical values in the region's interior.\n\nNext, we need to find the minimum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)=2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=4\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨2x,-4⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨4x,2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x &=4\\lambda x \\\\ -4 &=2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x(1-2\\lambda ) &=0 \\\\ \\lambda y &=-2\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- Assuming $x=0$ in the first equation of the system, the constraint condition $g(x,y)=4$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(0)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =4\\,⇒\\,y=\\pm 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(0,\\pm 2).$\n\n- Assuming $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{2}$ in the first equation of the system, the second equation of the system implies \n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}y=-2\\,⇒\\,y=-4.\n$$\n\n \nThen, the constraint condition $g(x,y)=4$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}+(-4)^{2}=4\\,⇒\\,{x}^{2}=-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich has no solutions.\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(0,\\pm 2).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,2)$ | $(0,-2)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $-8$ | $8$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at $(0,2),$ and its minimum value is $-8.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186495498 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186496336 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495498 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186496344 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495498 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186496354 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495498 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186496355 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495498 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186496379 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495498 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045464 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495498 #edb/ref 1045 "Optimizing Multivariable Functions Using Lagrange Multiplier