75 #edb/ref 17592186503225 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503629 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186503274 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503629 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503629 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503629 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186503069 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503629 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "e4d1887c-0c5d-5ac5-9c0f-77c952d10182" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503643 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186503145 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503643 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186503415 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503643 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186503427 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503643 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186503441 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503643 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186503466 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503643 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503643 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503643 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186503427 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503643 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "fbaaab81-294e-5a37-9fb1-b73c2d58a5c3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503661 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186503643 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503661 #edb/ref 1069 "q-67425" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503661 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503661 #edb/ref 1071 "Solve the equation $-\\frac{3}{x}=\\frac{9}{3x-1}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503661 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1a235d31-7002-5190-8de9-b78ca920ac5f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503661 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we note that\n\n- $x=0$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the left-hand side zero\n\n- $x=\\frac{1}{3}$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the right-hand side zero\n\nWe can flip an equation involving two fractions as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{c}{d}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{d}{c}\n$$\n\nThe above technique works whenever $a$ and $c$ are both different from zero.\n\nTherefore, we can flip the given equation as follows:\n\n$$\n-\\frac{x}{3}=\\frac{3x-1}{9}\n$$\n\nNext, we write both fractions using the least common denominator $9.$ The above equation becomes\n\n$$\n-\\frac{3x}{9}=\\frac{3x-1}{9}.\n$$\n\nWe can now solve this equation with the addition and the multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-9)\\cdot \\frac{3x}{-9} & =(-9)\\cdot \\frac{3x-1}{9} \\\\ 3x & =(-1)\\cdot (3x-1) \\\\ 3x & =1-3x \\\\ 3x+3x & =1-3x+3x \\\\ 6x & =1 \\\\ \\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right)\\cdot 6x & =\\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right)\\cdot 1 \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186576411] [17592186503684 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186503541 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503684 #edb/ref 1069 "q-67416" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503684 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503684 #edb/ref 1071 "Solve the equation $\\frac{2}{x-2}=4.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503684 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "528cc092-636b-5bd7-9846-07908de9113d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503684 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we note that $x=2$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the left-hand side zero.\n\nWe can flip an equation involving two fractions as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{c}{d}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{d}{c}\n$$\n\nThe above technique works whenever $a$ and $c$ are both different from zero.\n\nTherefore, we can flip the given equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-2}{2}=\\frac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nWe can now solve this equation using the multiplication and the addition principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{x-2}{2}\\right) & =2\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right) \\\\ (\\cancel{2})\\cdot \\left(\\frac{x-2}{\\cancel{2}}\\right) & =\\frac{2}{4} \\\\ x-2 & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ x-2+2 & =\\frac{1}{2}+2 \\\\ x & =\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186576411] [17592186503706 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186503571 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503706 #edb/ref 1069 "q-106354" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503706 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503706 #edb/ref 1071 "Solve the equation $\\frac{1}{x+5}=\\frac{3}{2x+12}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503706 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7e3eaae0-125c-585a-90f2-bbc8b4da35cd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503706 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we note that\n\n- $x=-5$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the left-hand side zero\n\n- $x=-6$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the right-hand side zero\n\nWe can flip an equation involving two fractions as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{c}{d}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{d}{c}\n$$\n\nThe above technique works whenever $a$ and $c$ are both different from zero.\n\nTherefore, we can flip the given equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x+5}{1}=\\frac{2x+12}{3}\n$$\n\nNext, we write both fractions using the least common denominator, $3.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\frac{3x+15}{3}=\\frac{2x+12}{3}.\n$$\n\nWe can now solve this equation with the addition and the multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\cdot \\left(\\frac{3x+15}{3}\\right) & =3\\cdot \\left(\\frac{2x+12}{3}\\right) \\\\ 3x+15 & =2x+12 \\\\ 3x-2x & =12-15 \\\\ x & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186576411] [17592186503715 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186503621 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503715 #edb/ref 1069 "q-67407" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503715 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503715 #edb/ref 1071 "Solve the equation $\\frac{4}{5}=\\frac{4}{3x}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503715 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9423a212-cd5b-5be8-a581-5c447b673232" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503715 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we note that $x=0$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the right-hand side zero.\n\nWe can flip an equation involving two fractions as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{c}{d}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{d}{c}\n$$\n\nThe above technique works whenever $a$ and $c$ are both different from zero.\n\nTherefore, we can flip the given equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{5}{4}=\\frac{3x}{4}\n$$\n\nWe can now solve this equation using the multiplication principle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{4} & =\\frac{3x}{4} \\\\ 4\\cdot \\frac{5}{4} & =4\\cdot \\frac{3x}{4} \\\\ \\cancel{4}\\cdot \\frac{5}{\\cancel{4}} & =\\cancel{4}\\cdot \\frac{3x}{\\cancel{4}} \\\\ 5 & =3x \\\\ \\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)\\cdot 5 & =\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)\\cdot (3x) \\\\ \\frac{5}{3} & =\\cancel{\\frac{1}{3}}\\cdot (\\cancel{3}x) \\\\ \\frac{5}{3} & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the solution is $x=\\frac{5}{3}.$" #edb/ref 17592186576411] [17592186503729 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186503629 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503729 #edb/ref 1069 "q-67422" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503729 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503729 #edb/ref 1071 "Solve the equation $\\frac{6}{x+2}=\\frac{3}{x}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503729 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b01bcc83-5d54-554e-8eb4-318389e1d142" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503729 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we note that\n\n- $x=-2$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the left-hand side zero\n\n- $x=0$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the right-hand side zero\n\nWe can flip an equation involving two fractions as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{c}{d}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{d}{c}\n$$\n\nThe above technique works whenever $a$ and $c$ are both different from zero.\n\nTherefore, we can flip the given equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x+2}{6}=\\frac{x}{3}\n$$\n\nNext, we write both fractions using the least common denominator $6.$ The above equation becomes\n\n$$\n\\frac{x+2}{6}=\\frac{2x}{6}.\n$$\n\nWe can now solve this equation with the addition and the multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6\\cdot \\frac{x+2}{6} & =6\\cdot \\frac{2x}{6} \\\\ x+2 & =2x \\\\ 2 & =2x-x \\\\ 2 & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the solution is $x=2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512089 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186512964 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512089 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186512965 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512089 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186512982 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512089 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186512988 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512089 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186512991 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512089 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045464 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512089 #edb/ref 1045 "Optimizing Multivariable Functions Using Lagrange Multipliers" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512089 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186512982 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512089 #edb/ref 1048 #edb/ref 17592186046644 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512089 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050688 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512089 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "96f786d0-7a4e-5202-b49a-8bf736b34184" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512964 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186512965 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512964 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1d937a32-c217-5b66-9900-161e09432569" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512964 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186364648 #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
